<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_6_1">
    <title>What Are Logarithms?</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    State the definition of the logarithm base <m>b</m> and rewrite an expression in logarithmic form as an equivalent expression in exponential form, and vice versa.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Evaluate logarithms, including the common logarithm and the natural logarithm, without a calculator when possible.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Use a calculator to approximate the value of a logarithm and identify the graphs and key properties of <m>y = \log(x)</m> and <m>y = \ln(x)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            When studying processes of exponential growth and decay in Chapter 5 we encountered equations of the type:
            <md>
                3000(1.0433)^t=6000
            </md>
            where the unknown is in the exponent.
            To solve such equations algebraically, we need <em>logarithms</em>.
        </p>

        <definition>
            <title>Logarithm Base <m>b</m></title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>b</m> such that <m>b\gt0</m>, <m>b\neq1</m> be given. Then for every <m>x\gt0</m>, the logarithm base <m>b</m> of <m>x</m>, denoted <m>\log_{b}x</m>, is defined so that
                    <md>
                        b^{\log_{b} x} = x.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            The logarithm, <m>\log_{b}x</m> is the <alert>exponent</alert> such that the base <m>b</m> raised to this exponent is <m>x</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            In other words, <m>\log_{b} x</m> is equal to a number <m>p</m> such that <m>b</m> to that number is <m>x</m>.
            The expressions
            <md>
                \log_{b} x=p \quad \text{and} \quad b^{p}=x
            </md>
            are equivalent; they are a logarithmic and an exponential version of the same statement.
        </p>

        <p>
            The <em>base</em> of the logarithm is <m>b</m>, so we say <m>\log_{b}x</m> is the <q>logarithm base <m>b</m></q>.
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute the following:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log_2 8</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log_3 \frac{1}{9}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log_{10} 0.001</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log_{e} (e^2)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We have the logarithm base <m>2</m> in this example. The value of <m>\log_2 8</m> is the exponent such that <m>2</m> to this exponent equals <m>8</m>, which is <m>3</m>:
                                <md>
                                    \log_2 8=3 \quad \text{as} \quad 2^3=8.
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The logarithm base <m>3</m> of <m>\frac{1}{9}</m>, <m>\log_3\frac{1}{9}</m>, is the exponent such that <m>3</m> to this power equals <m>\frac{1}{9}</m>. This exponent is <m>-2</m>:
                                <md>
                                    \log_3\frac{1}{9}=-2 \quad \text{as} \quad 3^{-2}=\frac{1}{3^2}=\frac{1}{9}.
                                </md>
                                This example shows that the <em>value</em> or <em>output</em> of a logarithm can be negative, meanwhile the <em>input</em> value cannot be.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The logarithm base <m>10</m> of <m>0.001=\frac{1}{1000}</m> is the exponent such that <m>10</m> to that power equals <m>\frac{1}{1000}</m>. Because we can write
                                <md>
                                    10^{-3}=\frac{1}{10^3}=\frac{1}{1000}=0.001,
                                </md>
                                then the logarithm is
                                <md>
                                    \log_{10} 0.001=-3.
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The logarithm base <m>e</m> of <m>e^2</m>, <m>\log_{e} (e^2)</m>, is the power of <m>e</m> needed to obtain <m>e^2</m>. That is,
                                <md>
                                    \log_{e} (e^2)=2.
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            <alert>Note:</alert> If the argument inside the logarithm is a more complicated expression rather than just a number or a variable, we use parentheses around it. Often we use parentheses even if the argument is just a number or a variable. In other words, <m>\log_{10}x</m> and <m>\log_{10}(x)</m> are two different ways to denote the same thing. However, <m>\log_{10}(x+3)</m> and <m>\log_{10} x+3</m> are <em>not</em> the same, as in one case <m>x+3</m> is inside the logarithm and in the other case only <m>x</m> is inside the logarithm. It would be more clear for us to write <m>\log_{10} (x)+3</m> rather than <m>\log_{10} x+3</m>, as the parentheses emphasize that <m>x</m> is inside the logarithm and the addition of 3 is not.
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute the following:
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log_{10} (-100)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log_{e} 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We are looking for a number such that <m>10</m> to that number is equal to <m>-100</m>:
                                <md>
                                    10^?=-100
                                </md>
                                Would it be <m>-2</m>? Definitely not as <m>10^{-2}=\frac{1}{100}</m>. There is no number such that <m>10</m> to that number is <m>-100</m> as <m>10^p</m> is always positive by properties of exponential functions.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We are asking: <m>e^?=1</m>. Since <m>e^0=1</m>, then <m>\log_{e} 1=0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            The last example illustrates the following properties of logarithms which hold for any base <m>b</m>:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>If <m>w</m> is negative or 0, <m>\log_{b} w</m> is undefined as <m>b^p\gt0</m> for any power <m>p</m>.</li>
                <li><m>\log_{b} 1=0</m> as <m>b^0=1</m> for any <m>b</m>.</li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>\log_{10}(5)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We are looking for an exponent such that <m>10</m> to this exponent equals <m>5</m>:
                    <md>
                        10^?=5.
                    </md>
                    Notice <m>10^0=1</m> so <m>0</m> is too small, and <m>10^1=10</m> so <m>1</m> is too large. The exponent that we are looking for is somewhere between <m>0</m> and <m>1</m>.
                    The graph of <m>y=10^x</m> shows that such a number exists.
                </p>

                <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_6_1_1">
                    <latex-image label="6_1_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-0.5,xmax=2,
ymin=-0.5,ymax=10.5,
xtick={0.6989,1,1.5,2},
xticklabels={$\log_{10}(5)$,1,1.5,2},
ytick={1,3,...,9},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
x post scale = 1.2
]
\addplot[domain=-2:2] {10^x};
\node[rpoint with label={-15:{}}] at (axis cs:0.6989,5) {};
\draw[very thick,dashed] (0,5)--(0.6989,5)--(0.6989,0);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>

                <p>
                    Clearly there is a number between <m>0.5</m> and <m>1</m> such that <m>10</m> to that number is <m>5</m>. That number is by definition <m>\log_{10}(5)</m>. What's the simplest way to approximate it? Use your calculator! Most likely you have two buttons on your calculator related to logarithms: <q>log</q> and <q>ln</q>. <q>log</q> is the logarithm of base <m>10</m>. We use our calculator and obtain:
                    <md>
                        \log_{10} (5)\approx 0.69897.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <p>
            While the logarithm base <m>2</m> has some importance in the theory of music and other applications, the applications we will focus on rely heavily on the <alert>common logarithm</alert> and <alert>natural logarithm</alert>, which we will define below.
        </p>

        <p>
            The logarithm of base <m>10</m> is denoted by <m>\log(x)</m> and called the <em>common logarithm</em>:
            <md>
                \log(x)=\log_{10}(x)
            </md>
            The logarithm of base <m>e</m> is denoted by <m>\ln(x)</m> and called the <em>natural logarithm</em>:
            <md>
                \ln(x)=\log_{e}(x)
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            To evaluate and manipulate these two logarithms correctly, you have to remember what their bases are:
            <md>
                <mrow>\log x \amp = \log_{10} x = p \amp \quad \amp \leftrightarrow \amp  \amp \amp 10^p \amp = x</mrow>
                <mrow>\ln x \amp = \log_{e} x = p \amp \quad \amp \leftrightarrow \amp  \amp \amp e^p \amp = x</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the following expressions without a calculator.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln e</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln \left(\dfrac{1}{e^3}\right)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln 0</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln (e^{-0.078})</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log(\log 10)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log(\sqrt{10})-\log(10^2)</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The natural logarithm <q>ln</q> is the logarithm base <m>e</m>. Since <m>e^1=e</m>, we have <m>\ln e=1</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                First, simplify the expression inside the logarithm:
                                <md>
                                    \left(\frac{1}{e^3}\right)=e^{-3}.
                                </md>
                                Therefore,
                                <md>
                                    \ln \left(\frac{1}{e^3}\right)=\ln (e^{-3})=-3.
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                This quantity is undefined. The natural logarithm, as any other logarithm, is defined for positive inputs only. There is no number <m>p</m> such that <m>e^p=0</m>, since <m>e^p</m> is always positive.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln 1=0</m> as <m>e^0=1</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                We have to raise <m>e</m> to the power <m>-0.078</m> to get <m>e^{-0.078}</m>. Hence, <m>\ln (e^{-0.078})=-0.078</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Because <m>\log 10=1</m>, then <m>\log(\log 10)=\log(1)=0</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Evaluate each term and then take the difference:
                                <m>\log(\sqrt{10})=\log(10^{\frac{1}{2}})=\frac{1}{2}</m> and <m>\log(10^2)=2</m>. Hence:
                                <md>
                                    \log(\sqrt{10})-\log(10^2)=\frac{1}{2}-2=-\frac{3}{2}
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use your calculator to find the approximate value of each of the following logarithms.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\ln 2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log 2</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>\log (e^{-0.078})</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Using the <q>ln</q> button of your calculator, <m>\ln 2\approx0.693</m>, rounded to three decimal places. As with many logarithms, <m>\ln 2</m> is an irrational number, so we can only have its decimal approximation. As a check, <m>e^{0.693}\approx 1.99970</m><mdash/>almost <m>2</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Using the <q>log</q> button of your calculator, <m>\log 2\approx 0.301</m>, rounded to three decimal places. As a check, <m>10^{0.301}\approx 1.99986</m><mdash/>practically <m>2</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Using a calculator, <m>\log (e^{-0.078})\approx -0.0339</m>.
                                To double check, we find that <m>10^{-0.0339}\approx 0.9249</m>, <m>e^{-0.078}\approx 0.9249</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
        <title>Graphs of Functions <m>y=\log(x)</m> and <m>y=\ln(x)</m></title>

        <p>
            The common logarithm <m>\log (x)</m> is defined for every positive input <m>x</m> and so is the natural logarithm <m>\ln (x)</m>. We can consider the two logarithmic functions <m>y=\log(x)</m> and <m>y=\ln(x)</m> with domains <m>x\gt0</m>. The graphs of the common logarithm function and the natural logarithm function have a similar shape:
        </p>

        <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_6_1_2">
            <latex-image label="6_1_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=0,xmax=4,
ymin=-2,ymax=2,
xtick={1,...,4},
ytick={-2,...,2},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
legend pos = south east,
]
\addplot[domain=0.1:4] {ln(x)};
\addlegendentry{$\ln(x)$};
\addplot[domain=0.01:4,red] {ln(x)/ln(10)};
\addlegendentry{$\log(x)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            We see that both functions <m>y=\log x</m> and <m>y=\ln x</m> are defined for all <m>x\gt0</m> and not defined for <m>x\leq 0</m>. Both functions are <m>0</m> at <m>x=1</m>: <m>\log 1=0</m> and <m>\ln 1=0</m>. Furthermore, both functions are positive for <m>x\gt1</m> and negative for <m>0\lt x\lt1</m>. We also notice that for positive inputs <m>x</m> which are closer and closer to <m>0</m>, outputs are getting <q>more and more negative,</q> and both graphs approach the <m>y</m>-axis without ever crossing it. We say that the <m>y</m>-axis is a <em>vertical asymptote</em> for <m>y=\log x</m> and <m>y=\ln x</m>.
            Recall that exponential functions have a <em>horizontal asymptote</em> at the <m>x</m>-axis. Finally, both functions are increasing.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <xi:include href="exercises/ex_6_1.ptx"/>
</section>
